    <string name="results_too_early">%1$d of %2$d confirmed days. The first evaluation is meaningful after %2$d days, until then everything here is preliminary.</string>
    <string name="results_nothing_yet">No clues yet. Keep logging, every entry is a piece of the puzzle.</string>
    <string name="results_why_title">Why no pattern yet?</string>
    <string name="results_why_too_few_days">A pattern only shows when something repeats. To see it, the app compares your days with each other and needs at least %2$d finished days. So far there are %1$d.</string>
    <string name="results_why_no_complaints">Your finished days had no clear complaints. Without them there is nothing for the app to look for, and that is good news. If something comes up again, log it.</string>
    <string name="results_why_few_complaint_days">So far you had complaints on one day only. Whether a food is behind them shows only when complaints happen more often and the app can see what those days had in common.</string>
    <string name="results_why_no_good_days">You had complaints on every finished day. The app finds triggers by comparing days with and without complaints. As soon as there are days without complaints, it can compare.</string>
    <string name="results_why_nothing_stands_out">So far no food goes along with your complaints clearly more often than others. Maybe it needs more days, maybe something other than food is behind them. Just keep logging.</string>
    <string name="results_tip_title">How to help the search</string>
    <string name="results_tip_text">Keep logging every day and finish it, also without complaints. Food you eat almost daily can only be tested if you leave it out for a few days now and then.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_leave_out_text">Important: the app cannot compare what you eat almost every day. Leave it out for a few days now and then and keep logging. Those days make patterns visible.</string>
    <string name="results_level_trace">🔍 Trace</string>
    <string name="results_level_suspicion">🔎 Suspicion</string>
    <string name="results_level_strong">🕵️ Strong suspicion</string>
    <string name="results_reason">Complaints afterwards %1$d of %2$d times, otherwise %3$d of %4$d</string>
    <string name="results_details">Show details</string>
    <string name="results_how_title">How this was found</string>
    <string name="results_how">After %1$s you had matching complaints %2$d of %3$d times. Without %1$s it was %4$d of %5$d times. The app counts complaints in the hours after a meal and compares both.</string>
    <string name="results_reason_days">Complaints on %1$d of %2$d days after, otherwise on %3$d of %4$d days</string>
    <string name="results_how_days">After %1$s you had complaints on %2$d of %3$d days. Without %1$s it was %4$d of %5$d days. The app compares whole days and checks whether complaints come the same day or up to 3 days later.</string>
    <string name="results_lag_0">Complaints mostly the same day</string>
    <string name="results_lag_fast">Complaints mostly within 2 hours</string>
    <string name="results_lag_1">Complaints mostly the next day</string>
    <string name="results_lag_2">Complaints mostly 2 days later</string>
    <string name="results_lag_3">Complaints mostly 3 days later</string>
    <string name="results_daily_title">Eaten almost every day</string>
    <string name="results_daily_hint">You eat these almost every day. Without days to compare with, the app cannot test them. Leave one out for 2 weeks to see whether you feel better.</string>
    <string name="results_how_lift">That is about %1$s times as often.</string>
    <string name="results_how_limit">A pattern in your entries, not a proof: other foods, sleep or stress can play a part too.</string>
    <string name="results_next_title">What you can do</string>
    <string name="results_next_strong">Leave out %1$s for one stretch (e.g. 14 days) and keep logging. If the complaints get rarer, talk to a doctor about it.</string>
    <string name="results_next_suspicion">Watch %1$s: log it every time, or leave it out for a while to see whether the complaints change.</string>
    <string name="results_next_trace">Too few entries yet for %1$s. Just keep logging; nothing needs to change.</string>
    <string name="results_next_lifestyle">%1$s seems to play a part. Note it in the day check and watch whether better days bring fewer complaints.</string>
    <string name="results_focus_text">Leave out %1$s</string>
    <string name="results_set_focus">Set as focus: %1$s</string>
    <string name="results_focus_set">Focus set: %1$s. A new stretch starts today.</string>
